        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 762,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 736,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Applies the scaled complementary error function - erfc(x) times e to the power x squared - to every element of a tensor.\n\nIt is how you compute with far-out tail probabilities without them underflowing to 0: carry erfcx and the e to the x squared factor separately, or take logs.\n\nTHE CURVE:\n1 at x = 0, then a slow decline, approaching 1 / (x times the square root of pi): 0.43 at 1, 0.056 at 10. Compare erfc(10), which is 2.8e-45 - the smallest number a 32-bit float can hold - and gives exactly 0 a little further on.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.erfcx\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it triggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is the same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device as the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nFor negative x it grows very fast (16206 at -3; about 2 times e to the x squared) and overflows to infinity before -10. Use it on the positive side.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200 evenly spaced values from its start to its stop (0 to 10 here), and the upper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is erfcx of each of those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from 0 to 10, drawn from 0 (bottom) to 1.05 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace to look at another stretch (start below -1 shoots off the top).\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.erfc is the unscaled version. t.special.i0e and t.special.i1e use the same trick (divide out the exponential) for the Bessel functions.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 720,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Applies the scaled complementary error function - erfc(x) times e to the power x squared - to every element of a tensor.\n\nIt is how you compute with far-out tail probabilities without them underflowing to 0: carry erfcx and the e to the x squared factor separately, or take logs.\n\nTHE CURVE:\n1 at x = 0, then a slow decline, approaching 1 / (x times the square root of pi): 0.43 at 1, 0.056 at 10. Compare erfc(10), which is 2.8e-45 - the smallest number a 32-bit float can hold - and gives exactly 0 a little further on.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.erfcx\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it triggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is the same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device as the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nFor negative x it grows very fast (16206 at -3; about 2 times e to the x squared) and overflows to infinity before -10. Use it on the positive side.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200 evenly spaced values from its start to its stop (0 to 10 here), and the upper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is erfcx of each of those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from 0 to 10, drawn from 0 (bottom) to 1.05 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace to look at another stretch (start below -1 shoots off the top).\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.erfc is the unscaled version. t.special.i0e and t.special.i1e use the same trick (divide out the exponential) for the Bessel functions."
        },
